This version avoids both recursion and an explicit stack. When a node has a left child, it finds the rightmost node in that left subtree and temporarily links it back to the current node. That link acts like a bookmark.

The current node is added when the bookmark is created, because the root must be visited before its left subtree. The temporary link is removed before moving to the right subtree, so the input tree is restored.

Dry run for `1` with left child `2` and right child `3`:

1. Visit `1` and create a link from `2` back to `1`.
2. Visit `2`, then return through the temporary link.
3. Remove the link and visit `3`.

The result is `[1, 2, 3]`.

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> preorder(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<int> result;
        TreeNode* current = root;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->left == nullptr) {
                result.push_back(current->data);
                current = current->right;
                continue;
            }

            TreeNode* predecessor = current->left;
            while (predecessor->right != nullptr &&
                   predecessor->right != current) {
                predecessor = predecessor->right;
            }

            if (predecessor->right == nullptr) {
                result.push_back(current->data);
                predecessor->right = current;
                current = current->left;
            } else {
                predecessor->right = nullptr;
                current = current->right;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Although some predecessor links are searched more than once, the total work stays linear.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned list. The tree is changed briefly while the traversal runs, then restored.

Recursion is not needed here. The stack stores nodes that still need to be visited. After taking one node, its right child goes in first and its left child goes in second. Since the stack is last in, first out, the left child is processed first.

For example, with `1` above `2` and `3`, the stack starts with `1`. After visiting it, pushing `3` and then `2` makes `2` come out before `3`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Stack;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> preorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return result;
        }

        Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            result.add(node.data);

            if (node.right != null) {
                stack.push(node.right);
            }
            if (node.left != null) {
                stack.push(node.left);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case for the stack and result. The stack is usually described as `O(h)` for the tree height.

This is another iterative form. It walks down the left side immediately, while saving only right children that must be visited later. When the current path ends, the next saved right child becomes the new path.

That means the stack does not store every discovered node. It stores the pending right turns. The empty-root case is handled before the loop.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Stack;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> preorder(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Stack<TreeNode> pendingRight = new Stack<>();
        TreeNode current = root;

        while (current != null || !pendingRight.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                result.add(current.data);

                if (current.right != null) {
                    pendingRight.push(current.right);
                }
                current = current.left;
            }

            if (!pendingRight.isEmpty()) {
                current = pendingRight.pop();
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since every node is reached once.

Space complexity: `O(h)` for the saved right children, where `h` is the tree height. The input tree is not changed.

