/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *      constructor(val = 0, left = null, right = null){
 *          this.data = val;
 *          this.left = null;
 *          this.right = null;
 *      }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {

    preorder(root) {
        const traversalArr = [];
        const stack = [];
        let node = root;

        while(true) {
            if(node) {
                traversalArr.push(node.data);
                // what if we directly add right node to stack as we need not come back to this node again
                stack.push(node.right);
                node = node.left;
            }else {
                if(!stack.length) break;
                // this node will directly be the right node where we want to go
                node = stack.pop();
            }
        }
        return traversalArr;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
**O(N)** where `N` is the number of nodes in the binary tree. We visit and process every node exactly once. Array push and pop operations run in O(1) time.

**Space Complexity:**
**O(H)** where `H` is the height of the binary tree. This space is used by the `stack` to store the right child pointers. 
*   **Worst case:** O(N) space if the tree is heavily unbalanced (e.g., a straight line of left children), meaning we push a `null` right child for every node. 
*   **Best/Average case:** O(log N) space if the tree is perfectly balanced.

